1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,912
Hi, this is Batas.
It's an Indonesian word.

2
00:00:03,912 --> 00:00:07,826
It means a mandate: authority with limits
you can't cross.

3
00:00:07,826 --> 00:00:13,200
If an AI agent runs your liquidity, what
actually stops it?

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:18,764
On 1inch Aqua, your tokens stay in your
wallet, and the app pulls them at

5
00:00:18,764 --> 00:00:23,330
settlement. Batas puts the limits inside
that swap. Watch the probe.

6
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:27,539
Inside the envelope, it settles.
Under the floor, it's refused.

7
00:00:27,539 --> 00:00:31,100
Past the size cap, refused again.

8
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,815
This isn't a simulation.
Here's the ship and a swap on Sepolia,

9
00:00:35,815 --> 00:00:39,654
real token transfers.
Aqua is the official contract, untouched.

10
00:00:39,654 --> 00:00:46,200
What I added is a router with two new
SwapVM instructions, 0x21 and 0x22.

11
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:51,879
It's tested. Forge runs seventy-two tests,
all passing. The harder one is a banded

12
00:00:51,879 --> 00:00:58,182
position. This test ships it to the real
Aqua on a Sepolia fork, trades both ways,

13
00:00:58,182 --> 00:01:01,700
and gets refused past every limit.

14
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:06,307
So who's allowed to trade?
An ENS name, agent.batas.eth, on ENSv2.

15
00:01:06,307 --> 00:01:09,870
I registered batas.eth, and its
subregistry is my own.

16
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:16,800
The name expires with the mandate, I can
revoke it, and nobody can transfer it.

17
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,431
Here's the key part.
The settlement reads that registry.

18
00:01:20,431 --> 00:01:26,199
Revoke the name and every swap is refused,
for every caller, not just my agent.

19
00:01:26,199 --> 00:01:31,500
This is the recorded run: quote, revoke,
refused, grant back.

20
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,511
The name has its own permissioned
resolver. A second account may write one

21
00:01:36,511 --> 00:01:39,900
text record, nothing more.
mandate.batas.eth is registered nowhere,

22
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:44,911
but it answers with the agent's records.
And other labels resolve through the

23
00:01:44,911 --> 00:01:46,600
parent.

24
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:51,298
None of this is hard-coded.
The live service resolves the name on

25
00:01:51,298 --> 00:01:56,220
every request, and checks its link to
ERC-8004 agent 10123, both ways.

26
00:01:56,220 --> 00:02:00,900
The agent is a namespace with its own
identity.

27
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:05,543
Now Hedera. This is a different agent,
with its own wallet.

28
00:02:05,543 --> 00:02:10,371
It finds the service in the ERC-8004
registry and checks the x402 manifest

29
00:02:10,371 --> 00:02:17,600
agrees. The checks are free, but in
paranoid mode it pays for the full answer.

30
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,800
That payment settled on Hedera testnet
through Blocky402.

31
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:25,972
The price is metered from the request, so
this call cost 0.0012 HBAR.

32
00:02:25,972 --> 00:02:29,900
And the operator's ERC-8004 identity
checks out.

33
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:34,187
Agents can negotiate, too.
My client asks for fourteen tokens.

34
00:02:34,187 --> 00:02:39,779
The Batas agent says that's over the cap
and counters with the largest size that

35
00:02:39,779 --> 00:02:44,104
clears the floor.
The client accepts and pays in the same

36
00:02:44,104 --> 00:02:45,800
task.

37
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:51,070
It doesn't have to be HBAR.
Here it pays with our HTS token.

38
00:02:51,070 --> 00:02:57,700
The token has a fixed custom fee, and it's
charged right in the settlement.

39
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:03,925
After every payment, the payer writes a
record to an HCS topic, with hashes of the

40
00:03:03,925 --> 00:03:07,525
request and response.
My checker verifies each record against

41
00:03:07,525 --> 00:03:09,600
the ledger.

42
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:13,523
Payments can repeat.
This order paid three times by scheduled

43
00:03:13,523 --> 00:03:18,668
transactions, and another was cancelled.
Other agents can also find Batas in the

44
00:03:18,668 --> 00:03:21,100
Hashgraph Online directory.

45
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:26,632
There's a Hedera Agent Kit adapter in the
package. The README covers setup, the

46
00:03:26,632 --> 00:03:31,792
architecture, and how the payment flow
works. And it's a hundred and forty-two

47
00:03:31,792 --> 00:03:34,600
commits over seven days.

48
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:39,036
Last, the live app.
Every number here is read from Sepolia,

49
00:03:39,036 --> 00:03:44,455
Hedera and ENS when the page loads.
The agent trades, the settlement holds the

50
00:03:44,455 --> 00:03:49,400
limits, and I can take the authority back.
Thanks for watching.
